# Handoff and commits

When you implement or materially change **CI workflows**, **quality gates**, **scripts used by callers**, or **docs/contracts** in this repo:

1. **Update** [`docs/ai-agent-handoff.md`](docs/ai-agent-handoff.md) so the next human or agent session reflects the new behavior (what changed, where, and any non-obvious decisions). Skip only for trivial typo-only edits.

2. At the **end of your final reply** to the user, include a **conventional commit** message suggestion (one line) in the form:

   `type(scope): imperative description`

   Use types such as `fix`, `feat`, `docs`, `ci`, `refactor`, `test`. Scopes examples: `ci`, `quality-gates`, `scripts`, `workflows`.

This project uses that format in git history; matching it keeps automation and reviewers aligned.

See also **[`radon-complexity.mdc`](radon-complexity.mdc)** — run **`radon cc -s`** on edited **`scripts/`** and **`tests/`** Python files; **`scripts/quality_gates/`** must stay at **CC ≤ 5** per function (no grade **B** on touched code).

See also **[`interrogate-docstrings.mdc`](interrogate-docstrings.mdc)** — CI **`interrogate scripts`** (≥ **95%**) applies only to **`scripts/`**; follow that rule when you touch **`scripts/**/*.py`**.

When you add or edit **`.github/workflows/*.yml`** or **`.github/actions/**/action.yml`**, run **zizmor** (or ensure CI would pass): **do not** embed **`${{ steps.*.outcome }}`** (or other expressions) inside **`run: |`** shell strings — pass them via **`env:`** and reference plain shell variables (avoids **template-injection** findings). Goal: **no zizmor findings** on touched workflow files.
